  "live_records": {
    "q-93744": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93744",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $5x+2y=75?$\n\nI. $(11,10)$\n\nII. $(10,15)$\n\nIII. $(5,25)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(11,10)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=11$ and $y=10,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =75 \\\\ 5\\cdot 11+2\\cdot 10 & =75 \\\\ 55+20 & =75 \\\\ 75 & =75\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(10,15)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=10$ and $y=15,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =75 \\\\ 5\\cdot 10+2\\cdot 15 & =75 \\\\ 50+30 & =75 \\\\ 80 & \\ne 75\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(5,25)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=5$ and $y=25,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x+2y & =75 \\\\ 5\\cdot 5+2\\cdot 25 & =75 \\\\ 25+50 & =75 \\\\ 75 & =75\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
