    "q-93736": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93736",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following pairs $(x,y)$ are solutions of the Diophantine equation $3x+6y=18?$\n\nI. $(10,-1)$\n\nII. $(4,1)$\n\nIII. $(-6,6)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's check each pair in turn.\n\n- $(10,-1)$ is *not* a solution of our equation. Substituting $x=10$ and $y=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+6y & =18 \\\\ 3\\cdot 10+6\\cdot (-1) & =18 \\\\ 30-6 & =18 \\\\ 24 & \\ne 18\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(4,1)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=4$ and $y=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+6y & =18 \\\\ 3\\cdot 4+6\\cdot 1 & =18 \\\\ 12+6 & =18 \\\\ 18 & =18\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $(-6,6)$ is a solution of our equation. Indeed, substituting $x=-6$ and $y=6,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+6y & =18 \\\\ 3\\cdot (-6)+6\\cdot 6 & =18 \\\\ -18+36 & =18 \\\\ 18 & =18\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
