    "q-93797": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93797",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Which of the following Diophantine equations have solutions?\n\nI. $6x+12y=15$\n\nII. $5x-10y=30$\n\nIII. $-8x+6y=10$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each equation in turn.\n\n- Equation I has no solution. We have $a=6,$ $b=12,$ and $c=15.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(6,12)=6\n$$\n\nand $6\\,\\text{⧸}|\\,15,$ our equation has no solution.\n\n- Equation II has solutions. We have $a=5,$ $b=-10,$ and $c=30.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(5,-10)=5\n$$\n\nand $5\\,|\\,30,$ our equation has solutions.\n\n- Equation III has solutions. We have $a=-8,$ $b=6,$ and $c=10.$ Since\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(-8,6)=2\n$$\n\nand $2\\,|\\,10,$ our equation has solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
