    "q-93807": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93807",
      "name": "Question 11",
      "problem": "Given that $(x,y)=(1,-2)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $3x+6y=-9,$ and that $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the smallest possible positive value of $x,$ what is the value of $\\overset{∼}{x}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  Moreover, if $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n({x}_{0}+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,{y}_{0}-\\frac{a}{d}t),\\,t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=3,$ $b=6,$ $c=-9,$ and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(3,6)=3.\n$$\n\nSince $(1,-2)$ is a solution of $3x+6y=-9,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n(1+\\frac{6}{3}t,\\,-2-\\frac{3}{3}t)=(1+2t,\\,-2-t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe smallest possible positive value of $x=1+2t$ is attained when $t=0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=1+2\\cdot 0=1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
