    "q-93810": {
      "dom_id": "step-q93810",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Given that $(x,y)=(-4,-7)$ is a solution of the Diophantine equation $7x-3y=-7,$ and that $(\\overset{∼}{x},\\overset{∼}{y})$ is the solution with the greatest possible negative value of $y,$ what is the value of $\\overset{∼}{y}?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall that a linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nhas a solution if and only if $d\\,|\\,c,$ where $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b).$  Moreover, if $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ is a solution to the equation, then the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n({x}_{0}+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,{y}_{0}-\\frac{a}{d}t),\\,t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\n$$\n\nHere, we have $a=7,$ $b=-3,$ $c=-7,$ and\n\n$$\nd=\\text{gcd}(7,-3)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $(-4,-7)$ is a solution of $7x-3y=-7,$ the general solution must be\n\n$$\n(-4+\\frac{(-3)}{1}t,\\,-7-\\frac{7}{1}t)=(-4-3t,\\,-7-7t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe greatest possible negative value of $y=-7-7t$ is attained when $t=0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{y}=-7-7\\cdot 0=-7.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
